---
/* The user-driven half of docs/PUBLICATION_RESTRUCTURE.md §3.
 *
 * The desk used to publish a share count computed against ITS OWN capital figure. That is the
 * single most advisory-looking output a research product can emit — "here is what you should buy"
 * rather than "here is the setup". The arithmetic has not changed; who supplies the capital has.
 * Rule 4's formula is ported unchanged into src/scripts/calculators.ts so the desk's published
 * methodology and this tool cannot disagree.
 */
import ToolPage from '../../components/ToolPage.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Why does the desk not just tell me how many shares to buy?',
    a: 'Because it does not know your capital, and a share count against someone else’s capital is worse than useless — it looks like an instruction. Henneth publishes the setup: entry, stop, target and risk per share. The size is arithmetic on top of a number only you have, so it belongs here rather than in the research.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Where does this formula come from?',
    a: 'It is Rule 4 of the desk’s own risk rules, applied to the desk’s own model portfolio, and it is ported here unchanged: size by stop distance so a stop-out costs a fixed fraction of capital, then hard-cap the position value at 8% so no single name can dominate. The same code path runs on both sides.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Why 1% risk and an 8% cap?',
    a: 'They answer different failure modes. The 1% decides what a single stop-out costs you — at that level a run of losses is survivable arithmetic rather than a crisis. The 8% caps what one company can be worth in the book regardless of how tight its stop looks, because a tight stop on a big position is still a big position when a stock gaps through it overnight.',
  },
  {
    q: 'It says zero shares. Is that a bug?',
    a: 'No, it is the answer. A zero means your stop is too wide for that risk budget at that price — the setup does not fit your capital. The desk drops such setups rather than publishing them. Widening the risk until a position appears is exactly what the rule exists to prevent.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does this account for brokerage, CGT or slippage?',
    a: 'No. It sizes the position; it does not model the round trip. PSX costs run roughly 0.15–0.5% per side depending on your broker, and the desk charges an estimated friction per stock inside its backtests rather than assuming a flat figure. Treat the output as the size before costs.',
  },
];

const related = [
  { href: '/tools/compound-interest-calculator/', label: 'Compound growth' },
  { href: '/tools/sip-calculator/', label: 'Monthly investment' },
  { href: '/tools/inflation-calculator/', label: 'Inflation' },
];
---

<ToolPage
  title="Position Size Calculator PSX — Risk and Stop"
  h1="Position size calculator"
  description="How many shares to buy for a fixed rupee risk, using the desk's own Rule 4: size by stop distance, then cap the position at 8% of capital. Free, nothing stored."
  path="/tools/position-size-calculator/"
  lead="Your capital, your risk tolerance, your stop. The same formula the desk applies to itself."
  calc="position"
  updated="2026-07-22"
  fields={[
    { field: 'capital', label: 'Your trading capital', prefix: 'Rs', value: 1000000, step: 50000 },
    { field: 'riskPct', label: 'Risk per trade', value: 1, step: 0.25, suffix: '%' },
    { field: 'entry', label: 'Entry price', prefix: 'Rs', value: 100, step: 1 },
    { field: 'stop', label: 'Stop price', prefix: 'Rs', value: 94, step: 1 },
  ]}
  faqs={faqs}
  related={related}
>
  <h2>Two constraints, and the tighter one wins</h2>
  <p>
    The first sizes off your stop: if you are willing to lose 1% of capital on a trade and the stop
    is Rs 6 below the entry, you can hold capital &divide; 6 &times; 1% shares. The second ignores
    the stop entirely and caps the position at 8% of capital by value. Whichever produces fewer
    shares is the answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second constraint is the one people skip, and it is the one that matters on a bad day. A
    very tight stop implies a very large position, and a large position does not respect its stop
    when a stock gaps through it overnight on an earnings surprise. The cap is what stops a
    well-reasoned trade from becoming a concentrated one.
  </p>

  <h2>A zero is an answer, not an error</h2>
  <p>
    If the tool returns zero shares, the setup does not fit your capital at that stop distance.
    The desk's own pipeline discards those candidates rather than publishing them. The temptation
    is to widen the risk until a position appears; that converts a sizing rule into a wish, which
    is the failure the rule was written to prevent.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this is a tool and not part of the research</h2>
  <p>
    Henneth publishes levels — entry, stop, target, risk per share — identically for every reader,
    on a schedule. It does not publish a share count, because a share count requires a capital
    figure, and the only honest capital figure is yours. That line is deliberate: it is the
    difference between research anyone can read and a personal instruction.
  </p>
  <p class="muted">
    Nothing you type here is stored, sent anywhere, or attached to any analytics event. Research,
    not advice — the desk never executes trades and this is not a recommendation to take a position.
  </p>
</ToolPage>
